Given two strings, check if these two strings are identical(same) or not. Consider case sensitivity.Examples:Input: s1 = "abc", s2 = "abc" Output: Yes Input: s1 = "", s2 = "" Output: Yes Input: s1 = "GeeksforGeeks", s2 = "Geeks" Output: No Try It YourselfTable of ContentUsing (==) in C++/Python/C#, equals in Java and === in JavaScript - O(n) Time and O(1) SpaceUsing String Comparison Functions - O(n) Time and O(1) SpaceWriting your Own Method - O(n) Time and O(1) SpaceUsing (==) in C++/Python/C#, equals in Java and === in JavaScript - O(n) Time and O(1) SpaceThe idea is to use equality operator (==) to compare both strings character by character. If the strings are identical, it prints "Yes", otherwise, it prints "No". 
            C++

    #include <iostream>
using namespace std;

// Function to compare both strings directly
bool areStringsSame(string s1, string s2) {
    return s1 == s2;
}

int main()
{
    string s1 = "abc";
    string s2 = "abcd";

    if (areStringsSame(s1, s2)) {
        cout << "Yes" << endl;
    }
    else {
        cout << "No" << endl;
    }

    return 0;
}


